      "notes": "TECHNIQUE DEMO (phase2-design 5.2): percussion and dynamics. Every drum's length comes from its VOLUME MACRO reaching 0, never from a note-off row - the auto-cut idiom - so the noise lane is nothing but notes and a volume column. POLYRHYTHM (preset-suite 9.1 recipe A): the hat lane runs a 3-row dotted-8th cell whose phase is carried across three frames - entry rows 0/2/1 at frames 1/2/3 (64-row frames: entry = (-64k) mod 3) - cited 1:0 -> 3:61, and it resolves back onto the downbeat exactly at frame 4, where the kit changes to open hats. TRESILLO (recipe C, 16th level): both pulse stab lanes and the triangle carry 3+3+2 - pulse 1 attacks at rows 0, 3, 6 and 8, 11, 14 of every bar (1:0 -> 1:62) with the volume column shaping the 3+3+2 accent as 13/9/11, while pulse 2 plays STRAIGHT 8ths underneath (1:0, 1:4, 1:8, 1:12 ...), so the two pulses are 3 against 2 for the whole A section. The triangle groups 6+6+4 at 1:0, 1:6, 1:12. METRIC SURPRISE: the Fxx events are the demo's subject and the piece's one metric event - F param 8 at 3:0 drops the engine speed to 8 ticks per row (112.5 BPM), F param 120 at 4:0 crosses the speedSplitPoint of 32 and sets TEMPO 120 instead (10 ticks per row, 90 BPM), F param 6 at 5:0 restores speed 6, and F param 150 at 5:48 restores tempo 150 so the state at the loop seam is identical on both passes, which rule 2 of the loop convention requires. bpmRange is therefore declared [89, 152], wide enough to cover the 90 BPM the param-120 tempo event reaches as well as the 150 BPM base - the same declaration 09-first-light makes for the same reason. GHOST NOTES: snare ghosts at vol 4-5 on the off-16ths between the backbeats (rows 7 and 15 of every bar) throughout A. PER-SECTION KIT VARIATION: A = 3-row hat cell, snare 39, ghosts; frame 3 = snare 41, the higher timbre; frame 4 = hats out entirely, hat-open on the off-8ths, metal (44, mode 1) on row 2 of every bar and the backbeat moved to row 11; the finale = hat cell back plus a tom fill. Four distinct fills: the intro's snare crescendo (0:52 -> 0:62), the frame-4 open-hat kit change, and the tom-led finale fill at 5:48 -> 5:57. Axy swell: A param 15 = A0f, a fast crescendo UP (the y digit is up - the direction is inverted on this engine) at 3:0, cancelled with A param 0 at 3:8. 7xy tremolo: 7 param 50 = 732 on the held stabs at 3:16 and 2:0, cancelled with 7 param 0 at 3:48 so nothing runs across the seam. Gxx flams: G param 2, a two-tick nudge, on pulse 2 at 4:8 and 4:24. Sxx chokes: S param 3 cuts a 6-tick row halfway at 5:0, 5:16, 5:32 and on the tom fill. Vxx on NOISE selects the LFSR mode, and the engine fact that makes it demonstrable is that Vxx is inert against any instrument carrying a duty macro - every bank drum has dut-noise-long - so the demo adds x-hammer-shop-anvil, a metal-envelope instrument with NO duty macro, and switches it between mode 1 and mode 0 at 5:0, 5:24 and 5:26. Non-diatonic colour: the raised third of the E major dominant (g sharp 4 = 68, g sharp 5 = 80) at 1:48, and a Neapolitan b flat major at 5:16. B param 1 = loop to frame 1, so the intro plays once. This piece is deliberately quieter than the album pieces because it is drums and short stabs with no sustained lead; the -26.59 dBFS measurement is the fixture, not a mix error. The suite-wide rmsRange convention: the measured two-pass mix RMS +-1.5 dB, declared per song because the design's [-20,-9] window is unreachable on this engine and the floor is -30 dBFS. Measured -26.59 dBFS, declared [-28.09, -25.09] - measured-snug like every other song, and above the -30 floor."
